        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What does METs mean on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              METs stands for metabolic equivalents. It shows how hard the current speed and incline are as a multiple of the energy an average person uses at rest. One MET is sitting quietly, so a reading of 5 means roughly five times your resting energy use. The console typically estimates it from speed and incline, not from anything measured on you.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many METs is walking on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Using the standard ACSM walking equation, a 2.5 mph walk on the flat is about 2.9 METs, 3 mph is about 3.3, 3.5 mph is about 3.7 and 4 mph is about 4.1. Incline raises it quickly: 3 mph at 5% is about 5.4 METs and at 10% about 7.4. These are estimates for an average person walking hands-free.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do you convert treadmill METs to calories?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Multiply METs by your weight in kilograms and the time in hours. For example, 6 METs for 30 minutes at 160 lb, which is about 72.6 kg, is 6 times 72.6 times 0.5, or roughly 220 calories. Subtract one MET first if you want only the extra calories above resting. Treat the result as an estimate.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is a good MET level on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on your fitness and goal. Around 3 to 6 METs is conventionally moderate intensity and 6 or more is vigorous, but the same figure can be easy for a fit person and very hard for a beginner. The talk test, heart rate and perceived effort are better guides to how hard a session is for you.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What are MET-minutes?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              MET-minutes are METs multiplied by minutes of activity, combining intensity and time into one number. Thirty minutes at 4 METs is 120 MET-minutes. The US guideline of 150 to 300 minutes of moderate activity a week is commonly framed as roughly 500 to 1,000 MET-minutes, since 150 minutes at 3 to 6 METs is 450 to 900.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are treadmill METs accurate?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They are reasonable estimates for an average person, not measurements of you. They assume typical movement efficiency, a conventional resting rate, hands-free walking and accurate speed and incline readings. Holding the rails, an inaccurate belt speed or the console guessing wrongly between walking and running can all push the figure off. Use it to compare sessions on one machine.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
